  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nBeing on the periphery of the upper-level ridge, southwesterly \nflow dominates the weather pattern through Friday. This pattern \nentails hot temperatures region wide and daily chances for \nisolated to scattered showers and thunderstorms for \nSouthwest/Central through at least Thursday. Severe thunderstorm \nchances remain low Wednesday and Thursday. However, with a hot and\ndry surface layer, we can't rule out some gusty winds with \nshowers and thunderstorms. This pattern also continues to advect \nin wildfire smoke from the Pacific Northwest, which will keep \nhazy skies and air quality degradation.\n\nHeading into the weekend, the upper-level ridge begins to breakdown. \nIncreasing southwest flow will help bring in the hottest \ntemperatures of the week the state. The ridge breakdown with a \ndry cold front passage will bring a relief to the heat for the \nfirst part of next week. However, with the ridge breakdown comes \ngusty to strong winds late Saturday into Sunday, especially along\nthe Rocky Mountain Front. Combined with low relative humidity, \nthis causes concerns for critical fire weather. This troughing \npattern won'tlast too long, as ensembles hint at another ridge \nbuilding in mid week.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nHeat This Weekend:\n\nThere is good confidence in impactful heat settling in Saturday. \nThe European Model Extreme Forecast Index shows a 0.7 to 0.95 east\nof the Continental Divide Saturday, meaning it will be unusually \nhot for this time of year. There remains some model and run to run\ninconsistency with how hot it will get. Another big question mark\nis the wildfire smoke and if an increase fire weather behavior \nacross the Pacific Northwest will produce more smoke and dampen \nthe high temperatures. Regardless, there remains good confidence \nin impactful heat Saturday, especially across North- central MT.\n\n\nWinds This Weekend:\n\nModels still hint at the increasing potential for gusty to strong\nwinds develop along the Rocky Mountain Front and North-Central MT\nlate Saturday into Sunday. Models have been consistently showing \na 50-70kt 700mb jet developing along the Rocky Mountain Front and\nNorth-Central MT plains late Saturday night into mid Sunday \nmorning. The timing of this jet is not the most ideal for high \nwinds, but good ridgetop stability suggests mountain wave activity\nwill be possible. Another concern is impact on recreation along \nthe Rocky Mountain Front/back country due to falling trees. Right \nnow, there's a 20-60% chance for 55 mph winds along the immediate\nNorthern Rocky Mountain Front Foothills. Another concern with \nwinds is increased fire weather behavior Saturday and Sunday with \nthe combination of critically low relative humidity. -Wilson",
